                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Waldhausen category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            equipped with a bit of extra structure that allows us to consider it as a presentation (via
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            such that the extra structure allows us to conveniently compute the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{K}(C)
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Notably a Waldhausen category provides the notion of co
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"}> fibration sequence
            s, which are crucial structures controlling
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{K}(C)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↪
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  A &amp;\hookrightarrow&amp; B
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  0 &amp;\to&amp; B//A
                  }
          %p
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow B
            a special morphism called a Waldhausen cofibration compute
            = succeed "s" do
              %em homotopy pushout
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            Using this, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            is an algorithm for computing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            spectrum of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Waldhausen in his work in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            introduced the notion of a category with cofibrations and weak equivalences, nowadays known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em Waldhausen category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with zero object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} cof
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{cof}
            of the cofibrations and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                  %mo .
                  %mi e
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w.e.
            of weak equivalences such that
          %ul
            %li
              %p (C1) all isomorphisms are cofibrations
            %li
              %p
                (C2) there is a zero object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                and for any object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                the unique morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to a
                is a cofibration
            %li
              %p
                (C3) if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\hookrightarrow b
                is a cofibration and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo →
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\to c
                any morphism then the pushout
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi b
                      %msub
                        %mo ∪
                        %mi a
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\to b\cup_a c
                is a cofibration
            %li
              %p (W1) all isomorphisms are weak equivalences
            %li
              %p (W2) weak equivalences are closed under composition (make a subcategory)
            %li
              %p (W3) “glueing for weak equivalences”: Given any commutative diagram of the form
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi D
                            %mtd
                              %mo ←
                            %mtd
                              %mi A
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↪
                            %mtd
                              %mi B
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mo ∼
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mo ∼
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mo ∼
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi D
                              %mo ′
                            %mtd
                              %mo ←
                            %mtd
                              %mi A
                              %mo ′
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↪
                            %mtd
                              %mi B
                              %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      D &amp;\leftarrow&amp; A &amp;\hookrightarrow &amp;B\\
                      \\downarrow^\sim&amp;&amp; \downarrow^\sim &amp;&amp;\downarrow^\sim\\
                      D' &amp;\leftarrow &amp;A' &amp;\hookrightarrow &amp;B'
                      }
              %p
                in which the vertical arrows are weak equivalences and right horizontal maps cofibrations, the induced map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mo ∪
                        %mi A
                      %mi D
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi B
                      %mo ′
                      %msub
                        %mo ∪
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ′
                      %mi D
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\cup_A D\hookrightarrow B'\cup_{A'} D'
                is a weak equivalence.
          %p
            The axioms imply that for any cofibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\hookrightarrow B
            there is a cofibration sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\hookrightarrow B\to B/A
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B/A
            is the choice of the cokernel
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\cup_A 0
          %p
            Given a Waldhausen category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            whose weak equivalence classes from a set, one defines
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_0(C)
            as an abelian group whose elements are the weak equivalence classes modulo the relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A]+[B/A]=[B]
            for any cofibration sequence
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\hookrightarrow B\to B/A
          %p
            Waldhausen then devises the so called S-construction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mo •
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\mapsto S_\bullet C
            from Waldhausen categories to simplicial categories with cofibrations and weak equivalences (hence one can iterate the construction producing multisimplicial categories).
          %p
            The
            %span.newWikiWord
              K-theory space
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/K-theory+space"} ?
            of a Waldhausen construction is given by formula
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hocolim
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo .
                    %mi e
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega\mathrm{hocolim}_{\Delta^{\mathrm{op}}}([n]\mapsto N_\bullet(w.e.(S_n C)))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is the loop space functor,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is the simplicial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]\in\Delta
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Then the K-groups are the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %ul
            %li
              The axioms of a Waldhausen category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              are very similar to the axioms of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
              in which the initial object is also terminal. One difference is that the weak equivalences in a Waldhausen category are not required to satisfy
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-out-of-3"} 2-out-of-3
              = succeed "." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  simple homotopy equivalences
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/simple+homotopy+equivalences"} ?
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
              is the axioms that every object has a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+object"} path object
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#waldhausen_category_of_a_small_abelian_category Waldhausen category of a small abelian category
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of bounded chain complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^b(C)
            becomes a Waldhausen category by taking
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
                of chain complexes;
            %li
              %p
                a cofibration
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mo •
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mo •
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A_\bullet \to X_\bullet
                is a chain morphism that is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                in each degree
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n : A_n \to X_n
          %h3#waldhausen_category_of_a_small_exact_category Waldhausen category of a small exact category
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+exact+category"} Quillen exact category
            with ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C
            there is an analogous, slightly more sophisticated construction of a Waldhausen category structure on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch^b(C)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                weak equivalences are the morphisms that are
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat C
            %li
              %p
                the cofibrations are the degreewise
                = succeed "," do
                  %em admissible morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to X
                such that the pushout
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to X \to A/X
                computed in the ambient
